oll_sd": 4.54, "poll_weight": 0.55, "fundamentals": {"margin": 7.12, "lean": -1.0, "environment": 7.56, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": 0.56}, "rating_margin": 9.6, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.76, "baseline_margin": 9.41, "analyst_adjustment": -0.21, "key_factors": ["Roy Cooper's large polling lead (~9-10 points) across many recent surveys", "Cooper's fundraising dominance ($35M raised, $20.7M on hand vs. Whatley's $3.5M)", "Whatley's campaign pausing TV ads while trailing, and GOP shifting resources elsewhere", "Open seat in a near-even state, with a Libertarian and Green candidate on the ballot"], "rationale": "Cooper leads the polling average by roughly nine points with consistent results across pollsters, holds a decisive money advantage, and markets price his win chances in the mid-90s. Most raters call the race Likely Democratic, so the forecast sits at the top of that band rather than Safe, reflecting residual uncertainty in a state with a near-even partisan lean.", "watch": "A late Whatley advertising resurgence funded by outside groups, or polling error toward the Republicans in a low-turnout electorate, could narrow the race considerably.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's open Senate seat has tilted sharply toward Democrat Roy Cooper since incumbent Thom Tillis announced his retirement. Recent polls from mid-September through early October show Cooper ahead by margins of five to eighteen points, with the aggregate around nine points. Cooper has also built a large financial edge, raising roughly three times what Republican Michael Whatley has and holding over $20 million on hand.\n\nWhatley's campaign has paused television advertising while trailing, and national Republicans appear to be redirecting resources to other states, though Tillis has endorsed him and outside groups have spent heavily against Cooper. Prediction markets put Cooper's win chances in the mid-90s, an